# .easignore — what EAS Build uploads
#
# Why this file exists: EAS Build uploads only the files it does not ignore, and
# when a `.easignore` file is present it is used **instead of** `.gitignore`
# (https://docs.expo.dev/build-reference/easignore/). Two files the build needs are
# deliberately not tracked by git, so they must be listed here with a `!` prefix,
# after the rest of `.gitignore`, or the Android build fails with
# `"google-services.json" is missing, make sure that the file exists` and the app
# ships without its Supabase URL.
#
# Keep this file in step with `.gitignore`: whatever is added there must be added
# here too, or it will be uploaded to the build servers. `tests/config/app-config.test.mjs`
# checks the two `!` entries below, because losing them produces a confusing build
# failure rather than an obvious one.

# --- everything from .gitignore -------------------------------------------------

# dependencies
node_modules/

# Expo
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
expo-env.d.ts

# Supabase CLI scratch state (project ref, pooler url, versions)
supabase/.temp/

# Native
.kotlin/
*.orig.*
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision

# Metro
.metro-health-check*

# debug
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.pem

# credentials — these stay OUT of the upload on purpose
.env.*
!.env.example
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
*-service-account*.json
google-play-service-account.json

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# generated native folders
/ios
/android

# --- files the build needs that git must never track ---------------------------

# Android build input: app.json points at it, and the Google Services Gradle plugin
# fails the build when the package inside disagrees with android.package.
# This covers `eas build` run from a machine that has the file. Builds triggered
# elsewhere (dashboard, GitHub) get it from the GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON project secret
# instead — scripts/provide-google-services.mjs (eas-build-pre-install) writes that
# secret to this same path, then verifies project/package/bucket either way.
# The file must come from Firebase project "mbkconnect" (docs/notifications.md §2b).
!google-services.json

# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY are read at build time
# and inlined into the bundle. Both values are public by design (the anon key ships
# inside the app), which is why uploading the file is acceptable. If anything
# private is ever added to `.env`, move the public pair to EAS environment
# variables instead and delete this line — see docs/notifications.md §4.
!.env
